text:
harper: Quick heads-up — discovered a tricky UX bug in the Go generator streaming sampleapps. When auth refresh happens mid-stream and users call Close()/cancel their ctx we sometimes hang or leak a goroutine. I added sampleapp/generator/stop-pattern-repro.go with a tiny repro. Anyone seen this?

matt: Yup. Symptom usually comes from the transport reconnect path racing with the shutdown path. If both sides try to close the same channel or send to it after it's closed you can get blocked sends or goroutine leaks.

dina: Short-term mitigation: keep an internal transportCtx separate from the user ctx and never close a channel from more than one goroutine. Also add an atomic state (enum) to indicate: active, draining, abandoned. That makes transitions explicit.

harper: Minimal pattern I used locally:

```go
// user opens with their ctx
stream, _ := client.Generator.Stream(userCtx, req)

// SDK internals
transportCtx, transportCancel := context.WithCancel(context.Background())
var state atomic.Int32 // 0=active,1=draining,2=abandoned
outCh := make(chan Event)

// transport reader
go func() {
  defer transportCancel()
  for {
    select {
    case <-transportCtx.Done(): return
    case ev := <-recvCh:
      if state.Load() == 2 { continue }
      outCh <- ev
    }
  }
}()

// on auth expiry -> SDK surfaces AuthExpired event with ResumeToken
// If caller wants to stop and persist progress:
state.Store(1) // draining
// SDK will wait up to DrainTimeout for in-flight events then set abandoned
```

matt: That looks good. Two ergonomics notes: 1) Go owners asked for explicit primitives rather than overloading ctx: add Stream.Abort() to indicate intent to stop now (doesn't cancel the caller ctx) and Stream.StopGracefully(timeout) to drain then abandon. 2) If we later add Resume(token) we must document that resume may replay the last N events; include Event.SequenceID for de-duping.

dina: Also surfaced in CI: generator_stop_drain_test flaked a few times. Filed https://ci/internal/stream-stop-842. I'll push a deterministic unit test that simulates auth refresh during drain so we don't regress. ci-bot: :robot_face: nightly: generator_stop_drain_test flaky; logs at https://ci/internal/stream-stop-842/logs.

harper: Proposal for Go SDK API change (sketch):
- GeneratorStream interface: Next() bool; Event() Event; Err() error; StopGracefully(timeout time.Duration) error; Abort() error; Resume(token string) error // future
- Event struct: Data string; SequenceID string; Type string // AuthExpired includes ResumeToken

matt: Implementation hints: make Abort() set state=2 (abandoned) and cancel transportCtx. StopGracefully should set state=1 and wait on an ack channel until either queue drains or timeout, then set state=2 and cancel. Avoid closing outCh in these methods; instead close in a single transport termination site.

dina: UX/docs: add a "Streaming gotchas" section in the Go README explaining difference between user ctx cancel (caller intent) and Abort() (stream intent) and show sampleapp patterns for persist+resume. Recommend persisting last SequenceID and deduping on resume because replay can occur.

harper: Action items: din a -> add unit test + CI ticket; matt -> open PR that adds Abort() + StopGracefully() stubs and internal transportCtx; harper -> update sampleapp with a clear StopGracefully pattern and a small de-dupe snippet; ops-bot: please skip flaky test generator_stop_drain_test while we land fix.

ops-bot: :white_check_mark: Skipping test generator_stop_drain_test on main until PR merges. See https://ci/internal/stream-stop-842/skip for details.

ci-bot: :robot_face: nightly rerun shows fewer flakes after skip; please link PR when ready for re-enable.

matt: One more thought — keep the Resume token shape simple (opaque string) and align with Node/Python teams. We'll add a small sync to pick exact header/handshake name.

harper: Thanks all. This should make the generator streaming UX much less surprising for new users and improve onboarding docs. :thumbsup:
